---------------------------------------------------------------------------
1  scripts/publish-release.ps1 - CREATED BY THIS CARD
---------------------------------------------------------------------------

  It did not exist, but two documents already assumed it did:

    Directory.Build.props line 99
      "win-x64 is passed at publish time by scripts/publish-release.ps1"
      - which is the stated reason RuntimeIdentifier is NOT set globally.
    plan.md section 2       lists it in the repository layout
    plan.md P1-03           deliverable: "publish profile / script"

  The tasks.md card does not mention it. Created here, so the props file's
  claim becomes true and P0-07's folder-structure box loses one blocker.

  WHAT IT DOES:
    - applies the win-x64 RID at publish time and nowhere else
    - takes -Component (Api|Maintenance|Procurement|Inventory|POS) and
      -Mode (FrameworkDependent|SelfContained)
    - REFUSES TO PUBLISH if PublishAot or PublishTrimmed resolve to true
    - deletes the target directory first, so a stale file from an earlier
      run cannot enter the package or corrupt the reported file count
    - records file count, size, and a package hash (section 5)

  WHAT IT DELIBERATELY DOES NOT DO. Spec section 18's "Versioning" row
  wants a full release: version number, commit identifier, migration list,
  release notes, rollback instructions, tested package hashes. This script
  produces a published folder and its hash, and stops. The rest is Phase
  6/7 packaging. A stub claiming to emit a release manifest would be worse
  than one that does not pretend to, and the script's own header says so.

  ONE GUARDRAIL WORTH NAMING. The AOT/trimming refusal is not decoration.
  Guardrail G-D reads .vbproj TEXT, so it cannot see -p:PublishAot=true
  passed on a command line, nor a value injected through an environment
  variable or a Directory.Build.props edit made after G-D last ran. The
  script checks the value MSBuild actually RESOLVED for this publish, with
  the RID and self-contained flags applied - the only number that decides
  what gets built. Neither works with Visual Basic (CLAUDE.md section 3).

