---------------------------------------------------------------------------
5  A DEFECT FOUND AND FIXED WHILE READING THE OUTPUT
---------------------------------------------------------------------------

  The script's first version recorded the SHA-256 of the primary
  executable, which is the obvious reading of spec section 18's "tested
  package hashes". The first two publishes returned:

    Api-fd\Merchandising.Api.exe   BBBFD640ADF26B33...   162,816 bytes
    Api-sc\Merchandising.Api.exe   BBBFD640ADF26B33...   162,816 bytes

  IDENTICAL. Checked further, and the managed assembly is identical too:

    Api-fd\Merchandising.Api.dll   0CC44E4A00DF1868...     7,680 bytes
    Api-sc\Merchandising.Api.dll   0CC44E4A00DF1868...     7,680 bytes

  WHY. The .exe is only the apphost shim, and the .dll is byte-identical
  because Directory.Build.props sets Deterministic=true. Everything that
  distinguishes the two packages lives elsewhere:

    Api-fd runtimeconfig.json:  "frameworks":         [...10.0.0]   <- REQUIRES
    Api-sc runtimeconfig.json:  "includedFrameworks": [...10.0.9]   <- CARRIES

  plus the ~330 runtime files present in one and absent from the other.

  A 0.2 MB package that requires a runtime on the host and a 105 MB package
  that carries its own would have been reported as THE SAME ARTIFACT. In a
  release process where the hash is what you check before deploying, that
  is the failure that ships the wrong package and looks verified.

  FIX. Package identity is now the SHA-256 over a sorted manifest of every
  file's hash and relative path. Paths are included because a package that
  moved a file is a different package; sorted so the result does not depend
  on directory enumeration order. The manifest is written BESIDE the
  package (Api-fd.sha256), never inside it - a manifest stored in the
  directory it describes changes the thing it describes.

  After the fix the two packages hash differently, as they must:

    Api-fd  562F2FDF8BB593CCA9FE40F9BDED814320E66E10F62905A0D6CD9AF87B58A1DB
    Api-sc  FDD9582C4BA88E5A84CA77E2BD0159B160D62C013E5754B0BE0720F32D86B29B

  Round-trip verified: recomputing the package hash from the written
  manifest reproduces the value the script printed.

---------------------------------------------------------------------------
6  THE ADR-010 DECISION, AND THE REASONING BEHIND IT
---------------------------------------------------------------------------

  API -> SELF-CONTAINED win-x64

    Spec section 18 permits framework-dependent "only with verified host
    runtime". This host does have Microsoft.AspNetCore.App 10.0.9 - BUT
    ONLY BECAUSE THE .NET SDK IS INSTALLED ON IT. The host laptop is
    currently also the development machine. A classroom or store host set
    up from scratch has no SDK and therefore no ASP.NET Core runtime, so
    the condition is not met in the deployment this decision is actually
    for. Reading the current dev machine as "the host" would be verifying
    the wrong computer.

    P1-16 raises the stakes: the API is installed as a WINDOWS SERVICE
    with automatic restart and must serve after an unattended reboot. A
    missing runtime there is not a startup error someone reads and fixes
    - it is a service that silently fails to start, discovered when a
    client cannot connect, plausibly during the demonstration.

    105 MB against 0.2 MB is a real cost, and on a laptop with a hard disk
    it is an acceptable one. It buys the removal of an entire class of
    deployment failure.

  MAINTENANCE UTILITY -> SELF-CONTAINED win-x64

    ADR-010's own rule is "match the API's choice", and there is a second
    reason that is stronger than symmetry: this utility owns BACKUP AND
    RESTORE (P1-17, P1-18). It is the tool you reach for when the system
    is already broken. A recovery tool that itself depends on a runtime
    being correctly installed is a recovery tool that can fail for the
    same reason you are running it.

  WPF CLIENTS -> FRAMEWORK-DEPENDENT win-x64, per the spec baseline

    Spec section 18 is explicit: "Versioned win-x64 framework-dependent
    packages, with .NET 10 Desktop Runtime prerequisite verification."
    Self-contained would be roughly 150 MB per client across three
    clients, to avoid one documented install step per laptop. The
    prerequisite is declared by the package itself (section 4) and belongs
    in the installation guide.

    OPEN VALIDATION, CARRIED TO P1-15 AND STATED PLAINLY. The prerequisite
    CHECK cannot be honestly tested on this machine, because this machine
    has the Desktop Runtime - a framework-dependent client runs here
    whether or not the check works. Proving it needs a client laptop
    without the runtime installed. What IS proven here is that the package
    publishes, launches, and declares the dependency correctly.

  FIXED REGARDLESS OF MODE: win-x64 throughout; PublishAot and
  PublishTrimmed never set, refused by the publish script and by G-D.

